 {
  "id": "L14/p1",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Which tokenization does GPT-style training use?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Characters, because every string is covered.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Whole words, with a special token for unknown words.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Pieces learned from data: frequent sequences of bytes become single tokens, and anything else falls back to individual bytes.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>A fixed list of English syllables.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Byte-pair encoding starts from the 256 possible bytes and learns merges of frequent adjacent pairs: \" the\" becomes one token, a rare name stays several. Every string is a sequence of UTF-8 bytes, so nothing is ever unknown. The vocabulary size is a dial between characters (small vocabulary, long sequences) and words (huge vocabulary, short sequences).</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/p2",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A model with a BPE vocabulary of 512 reports a validation loss of 2.4 nats per token, and a character-level model reports 1.6 nats per character on the same text. Which is better?</p>",
  "answer": "b",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The character model: 1.6 is less than 2.4.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>You cannot tell until you know how many characters a token covers on average.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>The BPE model, because tokens are better than characters.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>They are equal, because both use cross-entropy.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>The losses are per token and per character, different units. If a BPE token covers 1.9 characters on average, 2.4 nats per token is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.9</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.4/1.9 \\approx 1.26</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.4/1.9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.26</span></span></span></span> nats per character, better than 1.6. Divide by the characters per token, then by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">ln</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>, to get bits per character, the unit that compares models with different tokenizers.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/d1/s1",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "derivation",
  "prompt": "<p class=\"review-context\">Units</p><p>A loss of 1.5 nats per token with 2 characters per token is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5/(2 \\ln 2) \\approx</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.5/</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">ln</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span></span></span></span> <span class=\"blank\" data-blank=\"0\">[ 1 ]</span> bits per character.</p>",
  "answer": "1.082|tol=0.002",
  "answerKind": "expr"
 },
