  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>A bigram model with add-one smoothing is trained on the training split and evaluated on validation text. Compared with its training loss, its validation loss is:</p>",
  "answer": "a",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Higher, and the gap grows with the vocabulary size, because more bigrams are never seen in training.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Lower, because smoothing helps.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Equal, because a bigram model cannot overfit.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Infinite, because some validation bigrams never occur in training.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Smoothing keeps every probability positive, so the loss is finite, but bigrams that never appeared in training get only the smoothed share, and with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> possible pairs a larger vocabulary leaves more of them unseen. This is overfitting in its simplest form, and the reason language models are always evaluated on held-out text.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/p4",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>The capstone's tokenizer has a vocabulary of 512. What cross-entropy, in nats per token, does an untrained model have if it predicts uniformly?</p>",
  "answer": "6.25",
  "answerKind": "numeric",
  "tol": 0.1,
  "options": [],
  "explain": "<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln 512 \\approx 6.24</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">ln</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">512</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6.24</span></span></span></span> nats per token. The capstone's first logged loss is close to that (a randomly initialized model with small embeddings predicts nearly uniformly), which is a useful check that the loss is computed correctly before training starts.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/p5",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>You sample 300 tokens from a trained model at temperature 0.05. What do you typically get?</p>",
  "answer": "d",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The most fluent text the model can produce.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Random tokens.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>An error, because the logits overflow.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>A short phrase repeated over and over.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Near-zero temperature is greedy decoding: always the single most likely token. Once the model's most likely continuation leads back to a context it has seen, it loops. High temperatures fail the other way, sampling unlikely tokens until the text dissolves. Exercise 12 measures both with distinct n-gram ratios.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept1",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How does byte-pair encoding build its vocabulary?",
  "answer": "Starting from the 256 byte values, it repeatedly replaces the most frequent adjacent pair of tokens in the training text with a new token, recording each merge; encoding replays the merges in order.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept2",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why compare language models in bits per character rather than loss per token?",
  "answer": "Different tokenizers cut the same text into different numbers of tokens, so per-token losses are not comparable; dividing the total loss by the number of characters, and by ln 2, is.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept3",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What do temperature, top-k and top-p do when sampling?",
  "answer": "Temperature divides the logits, sharpening the distribution below 1 and flattening it above; top-k keeps only the k most likely tokens; top-p keeps the smallest set whose probabilities add up to p.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept4",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is weight tying in a language model?",
  "answer": "Using the token embedding matrix, transposed, as the output layer, so one matrix both embeds inputs and scores outputs; its gradient is the sum of both uses.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept5",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does byte-level BPE never fail to encode a string?",
  "answer": "Every string is a sequence of UTF-8 bytes and every byte is in the base vocabulary, so unknown characters fall back to their bytes.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L14/concept6",
  "lesson": "L14",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is perplexity?",
  "answer": "The exponential of the mean cross-entropy in nats; a model with perplexity k is as uncertain as a uniform choice among k tokens.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
